util: implement the disk cache on Windows (multi file cache)

The shader disk cache was a stub on Windows (disk_cache_os.c: "TODO: implement
disk cache support on windows") and meson refused -Dshader-cache=enabled there,
so llvmpipe compiled every shader variant of every program start again: about
180 ms of LLVM time on the draw thread for each new fragment or vertex shader
variant.

The multi file cache now works on Windows:

 - directory: %MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DIR%\mesa_shader_cache, else
   %LOCALAPPDATA%\Mesa\shader_cache (%USERPROFILE%\AppData\Local when
   LOCALAPPDATA is not set); MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DISABLE and
   MESA_SHADER_CACHE_MAX_SIZE work as on other platforms, and
   MESA_SHADER_CACHE_SHOW_STATS prints the hit and miss counts;
 - every directory the cache creates gets a protected DACL (no inheritance):
   full control for the current user, SYSTEM and Administrators only, the
   equivalent of mkdir(path, 0700), because the entries are native code that
   llvmpipe executes. An existing cache directory that is owned by somebody
   else (neither the user nor the owner that new objects of the process get)
   is not used: the cache is off for the process, with one message;
 - an entry is written to "<name>.tmp" under an exclusive LockFileEx() lock
   and moved into place with MoveFileExW() (no replace), so readers never see
   a partial entry and two processes that build the same shader do not both
   write it. The lock is on one byte far beyond the data, because byte range
   locks are mandatory on Windows and a lock on the data would make the reads
   of an entry that was just renamed fail. All handles are opened with
   FILE_SHARE_DELETE so that eviction never fails because another process is
   reading;
 - the size of the cache is kept in a file mapping of the "index" file that
   all processes share, with the same layout as the mmap()ed index of other
   platforms (put_key/has_key work too); entries are evicted oldest first
   (last access or last write time) when the limit is reached, and the size is
   recomputed when the index looks wrong. Rescans of several processes lose
   each other's updates, which let the directory grow past the limit, so every
   process also measures the directory again after it stored max_size / 8
   bytes; leftovers of killed writers are removed by those rescans after 60 s;
 - an entry that is too short, fails its CRC or its header check, has an
   uncompressed size above 64 MB or one that the data does not inflate to
   exactly, is deleted, so it is rebuilt instead of being a miss for ever
   (util_compress_inflate() now returns false for a stream that ends early,
   instead of an assert that release builds do not have). A read that fails
   for another reason (lock or sharing violation, I/O error) or a failed
   allocation is a miss and the entry is kept;
 - when the directory or the index cannot be created or opened (read-only
   volume, no permission), the cache is off for that process and nothing else
   changes.

util_barrier_wait() of the mutex and condition variable fallback (the one used
where there are no pthread barriers, such as Windows) returned true to every
thread, but its callers destroy the barrier on true as with
PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD. util_queue_finish() (disk_cache_wait_for_idle()
and disk_cache_destroy() call it) destroyed the barrier under the other queue
threads and hung with three or more threads. It now returns true to one thread,
the last one to leave the barrier.

The exit handler of util_queue runs from the DllMain of a DLL (libgallium_wgl.dll)
when the process ends, after Windows terminated the other threads of the
process; waking terminated queue threads is pointless there and blocked the
process for ever with an implementation of condition variables whose waker waits
for its sleepers. It does nothing on Windows now; a DLL that is unloaded while
the process goes on destroys its queues itself.

disk_cache_wait_for_idle() now returns at once for a cache that has no queue
(its directory could not be used); it waited for ever on the unused queue.

The parsing and writing of an entry, and the disk_cache_enabled() checks, are
the same code as on other platforms and moved out of the POSIX block. The
single file (Fossilize) and database caches are POSIX only and stay so.

